DX = ("CAT", "XAT")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Language and media
ps("V02-E-001", "Language and media", "Polite replacement words inherit the stigma of what they replace, because words take colour from attitudes.", "Medium",
   "Each generation seems to need new polite words for things that make people uncomfortable, and each new word eventually acquires the sting of the one it replaced. A term coined to soften the description of a disability or a humble occupation begins neutral, but because it is used to refer to the same people, held in the same regard, it gradually absorbs the attitudes attached to them. Within a few decades the once-gentle term is heard as an insult, and reformers propose another. This cycle does not show that changing words is pointless; a fresh term can briefly signal respect and break a habit of mockery. It does show that vocabulary cannot carry the burden of changing attitudes on its own, since words take their colouring from the attitudes they name rather than the other way round.",
   "Polite replacement terms tend to inherit the stigma of the words they displace, because meaning follows attitudes; renaming can help briefly but cannot by itself change how people are regarded.",
   [("Terms for disabilities and for humble occupations are especially prone to becoming insults, so reformers working in these two areas must keep proposing fresh and gentler vocabulary.",
     "Disabilities and humble occupations are only the paragraph's examples, not a special class, and the paragraph does not prescribe an endless supply of new terms; it says vocabulary 'cannot carry the burden' alone."),
    ("Since every new polite term eventually becomes an insult, efforts to replace offensive vocabulary are pointless and merely give reformers an illusion of changing attitudes.",
     "The paragraph explicitly says the cycle 'does not show that changing words is pointless', since a fresh term 'can briefly signal respect', so this option contradicts its qualification."),
    ("Words gradually shape the attitudes people hold, so a polite term that stays in use long enough will eventually soften the regard in which the people it names are held by others.",
     "This reverses the direction of influence: the last sentence says words take their colouring from attitudes 'rather than the other way round'.")],
   "The paragraph explains why polite terms keep turning into insults: they refer to the same people held in the same regard, so they absorb the existing attitudes. It then qualifies the point: renaming is not pointless, since it can briefly signal respect, but vocabulary cannot change attitudes on its own because 'words take their colouring from the attitudes they name'. The correct answer keeps the mechanism, the direction of influence and the concession.",
   "Choosing the option that calls renaming pointless, missing the explicit concession that a fresh term can briefly signal respect.",
   "V02E-euphemism-treadmill", "Two wrong options each catch half of the paragraph (the cycle, the direction of influence) but distort the qualification; the reader must hold both the mechanism and the concession.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

ps("V02-E-002", "Language and media", "A serious correction should reach roughly the audience the original error reached.", "Easy",
   "Newspapers often print an error on the front page and the correction, days later, in a small box near the back. Editors defend this as routine: corrections are numerous, and giving each one the prominence of the original story would crowd out the news. Yet the practice means that most readers who absorbed the mistake never meet the fix, so the record the paper leaves in its readers' minds stays wrong even after the paper itself has set it right. If a correction is meant to repair the harm an error caused, rather than merely to place the truth somewhere on file, it should reach roughly the audience the error reached. A serious mistake deserves a correction as visible as the story that carried it.",
   "Because a buried correction rarely reaches readers who absorbed the original error, serious corrections deserve prominence comparable to the stories they correct.",
   [("Editors bury corrections in small boxes at the back of the paper chiefly because they wish to hide their mistakes from readers and protect the paper's reputation.",
     "The paragraph reports the editors' stated reason, that prominent corrections 'would crowd out the news', and never attributes a wish to hide mistakes; the motive is outside the paragraph."),
    ("Every correction a newspaper prints, however trivial, should appear on the front page so that no reader is ever left with an inaccurate picture of events.",
     "The paragraph limits its demand to 'a serious mistake' and asks only that a correction reach 'roughly' the original audience, so 'every correction, however trivial' on the front page overstates it."),
    ("Newspapers print so many corrections that giving each one prominence would crowd out the news, so placing them in a small box near the back is reasonable.",
     "This is the editors' defence, which the paragraph reports and then rejects with 'Yet the practice means that most readers who absorbed the mistake never meet the fix'.")],
   "The paragraph states the editors' defence of burying corrections and answers it: a buried correction leaves the error in readers' minds, so if a correction is meant to 'repair the harm', it should reach roughly the same audience. Its conclusion is limited to 'a serious mistake'. The correct answer gives the reason and the scoped conclusion.",
   "Picking the editors' defence because it is stated clearly in the paragraph, without noticing that the 'Yet' sentence rejects it.",
   "V02E-newspaper-corrections", "The central claim is stated in the last two sentences and each wrong option is clearly off once the 'Yet' turn is noticed.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-E-003", "Language and media", "Subtitling and dubbing each lose something different, so preferring one is a judgement of what matters in a film.", "Medium",
   "Audiences who grow up with subtitled foreign films often regard dubbing as a betrayal: the actor's voice, half the performance, is replaced by a stranger's. Audiences raised on dubbing reply that subtitles betray the image instead, since the viewer's eyes spend much of each scene at the bottom of the frame and miss faces, gestures and composition. Each side is right about what the other loses and quiet about what its own method loses. Subtitles preserve the sound of a performance but condense its words, often dropping a good deal of the dialogue to suit the reading speed of viewers; dubbing keeps more of the dialogue and the whole image but sacrifices the voice. Neither is a transparent window onto the original. The choice is between two different losses, and preferring one is a judgement about which part of a film matters most, not a recognition that one method is faithful and the other is not.",
   "Subtitling and dubbing each sacrifice a different part of a film, so preferring one reflects a view about what matters most in a film rather than which method is faithful.",
   [("Subtitling is the more faithful method, because it preserves the actor's voice, which is half of the performance, while dubbing replaces that voice with a stranger's.",
     "This is the view of one camp that the paragraph reports; the author says each side is 'quiet about what its own method loses' and that neither is 'a transparent window onto the original'."),
    ("Viewers of subtitled films miss much of the image and much of the dialogue, so dubbing, which keeps both of these, is generally the better way to present foreign cinema to audiences.",
     "The paragraph notes that dubbing 'sacrifices the voice' and refuses to rank the methods, concluding that the choice is 'between two different losses'; this option takes the dubbing camp's side."),
    ("Because no translation of a film can be faithful, audiences should learn foreign languages and watch films in the original without subtitles or dubbing.",
     "The paragraph makes no recommendation about learning languages; the prescription is an outside idea added to its observation that neither method is transparent.")],
   "The paragraph sets out the complaint each camp makes about the other method, then judges that 'each side is right about what the other loses and quiet about what its own method loses'. Its conclusion is that the choice is 'between two different losses' and reflects 'which part of a film matters most'. The correct answer states the symmetry and the conclusion.",
   "Siding with one camp because its complaint is presented as correct, without seeing that the author grants both complaints and ranks neither method.",
   "V02E-subtitling-versus-dubbing", "Two wrong options each repeat a complaint the author accepts as true, so the reader must see that the paragraph's point is the symmetry, not either complaint.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

ps("V02-E-004", "Language and media", "Untranslatability lies in associations and tone, a matter of degree affecting ordinary words, not in missing single words.", "Difficult",
   "Lists of 'untranslatable' words circulate widely, each entry presented as proof that some culture has grasped a feeling the rest of us cannot even name. The charm of such lists rests on a confusion. That a language has no single word for something does not mean its speakers lack the concept; a phrase of five words can capture what another language packs into one, and the lists themselves translate every entry in a sentence. What is genuinely hard to carry across is different: the associations a word has gathered through use, the contexts where it feels natural, the tone it strikes in a joke or a lament. These are real losses, but they afflict ordinary words as much as exotic ones, and they are a matter of degree rather than an absolute barrier. The lists, by celebrating a handful of curiosities, end up obscuring where the real difficulty of translation lies.",
   "Lacking a single equivalent word does not make a concept untranslatable; the real difficulty lies in a word's accumulated associations and tone, a partial loss that affects common words too.",
   [("Since the associations and tone of words can never be fully carried across, every translation fails absolutely, and lists of untranslatable words merely highlight the most striking cases.",
     "The paragraph calls these losses 'a matter of degree rather than an absolute barrier', so 'fails absolutely' contradicts it; it also says the lists obscure, not highlight, the real difficulty."),
    ("The popular lists of untranslatable words refute themselves, since each of them translates every one of its entries in a sentence of ordinary prose.",
     "This is one supporting detail from the third sentence; it leaves out the paragraph's positive claim about where the real difficulty of translation lies."),
    ("Words that have no single equivalent in other languages are the hardest to translate, because they carry associations and tones that ordinary words lack.",
     "The paragraph says the losses of association and tone 'afflict ordinary words as much as exotic ones', so tying them specially to words without equivalents distorts the point.")],
   "The paragraph first rejects the idea behind 'untranslatable' lists (no single word does not mean no concept, and the lists translate every entry), then locates the real difficulty in associations, contexts and tone, adding two qualifications: these losses affect ordinary words equally and are 'a matter of degree'. The correct answer keeps both the rejection and the relocated, qualified difficulty.",
   "Choosing the option about words without equivalents, which sounds like the paragraph's vocabulary but reattaches the difficulty to exactly the words the paragraph says are not special.",
   "V02E-untranslatable-words", "The paragraph makes a negative claim and a qualified positive one; two wrong options use its exact terms (associations, tone, lists) while inverting a qualification, so the correct answer must be found by scope.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Education
ps("V02-E-005", "Education", "Smaller classes help under the right conditions and at a cost, not reliably everywhere.", "Medium",
   "Research on smaller classes is often summarised as showing that reducing class size raises achievement, and some carefully designed trials do find gains, especially for younger pupils and those from poorer homes. But the finding is easily stretched. The gains appear when classes shrink substantially and when there are enough capable teachers to staff the new classes; a large programme that must hire quickly may fill many rooms with inexperienced teachers and lose much of the benefit. Smaller classes are also expensive, and the same money might buy other things, such as tutoring or better materials. The evidence supports smaller classes as one useful option under the right conditions, not as a reform that reliably pays off wherever it is tried.",
   "Smaller classes can raise achievement, notably for young and poorer pupils, but the gain depends on large cuts and capable teachers and must be weighed against other uses of money.",
   [("Reducing class size reliably raises achievement for all pupils, which is why school systems should make it their first priority whenever new funds become available.",
     "The paragraph ends by denying that smaller classes are 'a reform that reliably pays off wherever it is tried', and it suggests the money might go elsewhere, so this contradicts it."),
    ("Large class-size programmes fail because quick hiring fills classrooms with inexperienced teachers, so tutoring and better materials are always the wiser use of school budgets.",
     "The paragraph says such programmes 'may' lose 'much of the benefit' and that money 'might' buy other things; 'fail' and 'always the wiser use' turn hedged possibilities into absolutes."),
    ("Trials of smaller classes find gains mainly for younger pupils and for those from poorer homes, which shows that older and better-off children gain nothing at all from smaller classes.",
     "The paragraph says gains appear 'especially' for these groups, which does not imply that other children gain 'nothing'; the option turns a difference of degree into an exclusion.")],
   "The paragraph accepts the finding that smaller classes can raise achievement, especially for some pupils, then limits it: the gains need substantial cuts and capable teachers, and the cost must be compared with alternatives. It concludes that smaller classes are 'one useful option under the right conditions'. The correct answer states the finding together with both limits.",
   "Choosing the option that warns about rushed hiring, which is a real point in the paragraph but stated there with 'may' and 'might', not as certain failure.",
   "V02E-class-size-reduction", "A finding-and-qualification paragraph where two wrong options each harden one hedge ('may', 'especially') into an absolute; the correct answer must keep the hedges.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-E-006", "Education", "Early lessons in the home language aid basic learning without blocking the school language.", "Easy",
   "Where the language of the classroom differs from the language of the home, a common policy is to plunge young pupils into the former from their first day, in the belief that early and total immersion produces fluency fastest. The belief mistakes the job of the early grades. Those years are spent acquiring literacy, number sense and the habit of inquiry, and every one of these comes harder when the medium of teaching is itself a puzzle. A pupil labouring over the sounds of unfamiliar words has little attention left for their sense. Beginning in the home language and bringing in the school language gradually need not hold back fluency in it; children who read confidently in one language generally find that the skill transfers to another.",
   "Early lessons work better in the language children know, with the school language phased in, because basic skills come more easily that way and later transfer.",
   [("Children should never be taught in any language other than their home language, since doing so permanently damages their capacity to learn to read and to reason.",
     "The paragraph recommends 'bringing in the school language gradually', not avoiding it, and it never claims permanent damage; 'never' and 'permanently' overstate it."),
    ("Early and total immersion in the school language is the fastest route to fluency in it, although it may make the first years of schooling harder for young children.",
     "This restates 'the belief' that the second sentence says 'mistakes the job of the early grades'; the paragraph argues against it."),
    ("Young children find it hard to decode unfamiliar words, which is why reading should be taught only after they have acquired a large spoken vocabulary.",
     "The paragraph uses the pupil 'labouring over the sounds of unfamiliar words' to argue for teaching in the home language, not for delaying reading; the prescription about spoken vocabulary is not in it.")],
   "The paragraph challenges the belief that early immersion is fastest: literacy, number sense and inquiry come harder when the medium of teaching is 'itself a puzzle', and starting in the home language 'need not hold back fluency' in the school language, because reading skill 'transfers to another'. The correct answer captures the recommendation, its gradual nature and the reason.",
   "Picking the immersion option because it is stated plainly in the first sentence, without noticing that the paragraph calls it a belief that 'mistakes the job of the early grades'.",
   "V02E-mother-tongue-instruction", "The argument is direct and each wrong option is clearly an overstatement, the rejected belief or an added prescription.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-E-007", "Education", "Uniforms do not hide wealth differences, so they must be defended on other grounds.", "Easy",
   "Supporters of school uniforms often claim that they erase differences of wealth among pupils, since everyone wears the same clothes. The claim is weaker than it sounds. Children are expert readers of status, and when clothing is fixed they turn to whatever is left: shoes, bags, phones, haircuts, the holidays they describe on Monday morning. Uniforms can also be costly in their own right when they must be bought from a single supplier. None of this means uniforms are without value; they may build a sense of belonging or simplify mornings for families. But if they are defended, it should be on such grounds, not on the promise that they make inequality invisible.",
   "Uniforms cannot hide differences of wealth, because children turn to other markers of status, so uniforms should be defended on other grounds such as belonging or convenience.",
   [("Because children always find other markers of status, school uniforms serve no useful purpose, and schools would do better to abolish them entirely.",
     "The paragraph says 'None of this means uniforms are without value' and names belonging and simpler mornings, so 'no useful purpose' contradicts its concession."),
    ("Uniforms that must be bought from a single supplier can be expensive, which places a heavy burden on poorer families and defeats the purpose of introducing them.",
     "Cost is one subsidiary point in the fourth sentence; the option misses the main argument about children finding other status markers and the grounds on which uniforms may be defended."),
    ("Uniforms build a sense of belonging and simplify mornings for families, which makes them valuable even though some children dislike having to wear them.",
     "This keeps only the concession and adds children's dislike, which the paragraph never mentions; it drops the central claim that uniforms do not hide inequality.")],
   "The paragraph rejects the claim that uniforms erase wealth differences, because children find other status markers, concedes that uniforms may have other value, and concludes that they should be defended on those other grounds. The correct answer keeps the rejection, its reason and the redirected defence.",
   "Choosing the option that dismisses uniforms altogether, overlooking the concession 'None of this means uniforms are without value'.",
   "V02E-school-uniforms", "A clear critique with a stated concession; once the 'None of this means' sentence is read, the wrong options are plainly too strong or too narrow.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-E-008", "Education", "The case against lectures is a case against bad lectures; at best they model thinking.", "Difficult",
   "The lecture is now routinely dismissed as a relic: students sit passively while an expert recites what they could read in a book. Much of this criticism is deserved when a lecture is merely a recitation. But it misdescribes the form at its best. A good lecturer does not transmit information so much as demonstrate a way of thinking, showing which questions matter, where the evidence is thin, how an argument can be tested and abandoned. Listeners watch a mind at work in real time, something a textbook, which presents only finished conclusions, cannot easily offer. The case against lectures is therefore really a case against bad lectures, and replacing the form with activities may discard what it does well while curing what it does badly.",
   "Criticism of lectures fits mere recitation; at its best a lecture models a way of thinking that finished texts cannot easily convey, so abandoning the form risks losing that value.",
   [("Lectures are relics in which students passively receive information they could read in books, and they should be replaced by activities that require students to think.",
     "This is the dismissive view the paragraph reports in its first sentence and then says 'misdescribes the form at its best'; the author warns that replacing lectures 'may discard what it does well'."),
    ("Good lectures and good textbooks serve the same purpose of showing how arguments are tested, so the choice between them is largely a matter of student preference.",
     "The paragraph contrasts the two: a lecture shows 'a mind at work in real time', while a textbook 'presents only finished conclusions', so treating them as equivalent erases the paragraph's central distinction."),
    ("Lectures are valuable because they transmit information more efficiently than books, letting an expert present in an hour what students would take days to read.",
     "The paragraph says a good lecturer 'does not transmit information so much as demonstrate a way of thinking'; locating the value in efficient transmission reverses its claim.")],
   "The paragraph grants that criticism of lectures is deserved for mere recitation, then argues that at its best the lecture demonstrates a way of thinking that a textbook of 'finished conclusions' cannot easily offer. Its conclusion is that the case is against bad lectures, and that replacing the form risks discarding what it does well. The correct answer keeps the concession, the distinctive value and the warning.",
   "Choosing the information-efficiency option, which defends lectures and so seems to match the author's side, but rests the defence on the very function the author says is not the point.",
   "V02E-lecture-defence", "Two wrong options defend lectures as the author does but on grounds the paragraph rejects or blurs (efficiency, equivalence with textbooks), so the reader must identify the precise ground of the defence.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Health and medicine
ps("V02-E-009", "Health and medicine", "Surgical checklists work by occasioning team communication, not by themselves.", "Medium",
   "When hospitals introduce short checklists before surgery, asking teams to confirm the patient's identity, the planned procedure and the availability of equipment, complications often fall. It is tempting to conclude that the checklist itself does the work. Yet the same list has produced little benefit in hospitals where it was imposed from above and ticked off by one person while others carried on preparing. Where it helps, it seems to do so by making the team pause together, by giving junior staff a recognised moment to speak up, and by turning silent assumptions into stated ones. The checklist is less a tool that prevents errors than an occasion for the kind of communication that does.",
   "Surgical checklists cut complications mainly where they prompt genuine team communication, the benefit seeming to come from the conversation they occasion rather than the list.",
   [("Surgical checklists reliably reduce complications because they force teams to confirm the patient's identity, the planned procedure and the availability of equipment before every operation.",
     "This is the 'tempting' conclusion that 'the checklist itself does the work', which the paragraph rejects by noting that the same list 'produced little benefit' where it was merely ticked off."),
    ("Checklists imposed by hospital managers fail because senior surgeons resent them, so they should be designed and introduced by junior staff instead of administrators.",
     "The paragraph never mentions surgeons' resentment or who should design checklists; it says junior staff gain 'a recognised moment to speak up', which is a different point."),
    ("Because checklists are often ticked off mechanically, hospitals would gain more by training surgical teams to communicate than by using any checklist at all.",
     "The paragraph says a checklist can help as 'an occasion' for communication, so it does not recommend dropping checklists; the option adds a prescription that contradicts this.")],
   "The paragraph reports a finding (complications often fall with checklists), rejects the obvious explanation by citing hospitals where the list 'produced little benefit', and relocates the cause in the team communication the checklist occasions. The correct answer states the conditional benefit and its real source.",
   "Choosing the option that drops checklists for communication training, which over-reads 'less a tool' as 'no use'.",
   "V02E-surgical-checklists", "The tempting wrong option states the paragraph's own opening finding; the reader must notice that the paragraph's point is the qualification of that finding.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

ps("V02-E-010", "Health and medicine", "Expecting side effects can cause them, so risks must be disclosed carefully, not hidden.", "Easy",
   "Patients in drug trials who receive only a dummy pill sometimes report the very side effects listed on the consent form: headaches, nausea, fatigue. This is the nocebo effect, the shadow of the better-known placebo effect. Expecting harm can produce real symptoms, partly because anxious attention makes people notice and interpret ordinary bodily sensations as signs of illness, and partly because worry itself can cause discomfort. The phenomenon puts doctors in an awkward position. They are obliged to warn patients about possible side effects, yet the way the warning is delivered may help bring those effects about. The aim is not to hide risks but to describe them in a way that informs without needlessly alarming.",
   "Expecting side effects can produce symptoms, so doctors must disclose risks but should describe them in a way that informs without needless alarm.",
   [("Because warnings about side effects can produce the very symptoms they describe, doctors should generally avoid mentioning minor risks to those patients who seem anxious.",
     "The paragraph says doctors 'are obliged to warn' and that 'The aim is not to hide risks', so recommending silence about risks contradicts it."),
    ("Patients who take dummy pills in trials often report headaches, nausea and fatigue, which shows that most reported side effects of real drugs are imaginary.",
     "The paragraph says expectation can produce 'real symptoms' and makes no claim about the proportion of real drugs' side effects; 'most' and 'imaginary' go beyond and against it."),
    ("The nocebo effect is the counterpart of the placebo effect and arises because anxious patients notice ordinary bodily sensations that they would otherwise ignore.",
     "This gives only part of the mechanism and omits the paragraph's main point, the dilemma for doctors and how warnings should be delivered.")],
   "The paragraph explains the nocebo effect and its causes, then draws the practical consequence: doctors must warn about side effects, but how they warn matters, so the aim is to inform 'without needlessly alarming'. The correct answer states the phenomenon and the qualified conclusion.",
   "Choosing the option about withholding warnings, missing the explicit 'not to hide risks'.",
   "V02E-nocebo-effect", "The conclusion is stated in the last sentence and each wrong option is clearly an overstatement, a contradiction or a fragment.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-E-011", "Health and medicine", "BMI's flaws lie in applying a population measure to individuals.", "Medium",
   "Body mass index, a person's weight divided by the square of their height, is often attacked as a crude and misleading measure of health. The attack is fair when the number is applied to individuals without further thought: a muscular athlete can register as overweight, and the measure says nothing about where fat is carried, which matters for risk. But the index was never well suited to judging single bodies. Its strength lies in populations, where it is cheap, consistent and reasonably correlated with outcomes across large groups, making it useful for tracking trends over time. The fault lies less in the measure than in asking it to do a job, the assessment of one patient, for which other information is needed.",
   "Body mass index misleads when used alone to judge individuals, but it remains useful for tracking health trends across populations, so the main problem lies in how it is applied.",
   [("Body mass index is crude and misleading, since it labels muscular athletes as overweight and ignores where fat is carried, so it should be abandoned in favour of better measures of health.",
     "The paragraph grants these flaws only for individual use and defends the index for populations; abandoning it altogether contradicts 'The fault lies less in the measure'."),
    ("Body mass index is a reliable guide to an individual patient's health risk, provided that doctors make suitable allowances for muscular patients such as athletes.",
     "The paragraph says the index 'was never well suited to judging single bodies' and that assessing one patient needs 'other information', so it is not a reliable individual guide."),
    ("Because body mass index is cheap and consistent, it is the best available measure of health for both individuals and populations, despite some minor flaws.",
     "The paragraph confines the index's strength to populations and calls its individual flaws real, so 'best' for individuals and 'minor flaws' both overstate its defence.")],
   "The paragraph accepts the criticism of BMI for individual use, then argues that its proper domain is populations, where it is cheap, consistent and useful for trends. It concludes that the fault is in 'asking it to do a job' it was never suited to. The correct answer keeps the concession, the defended use and the conclusion.",
   "Choosing the 'abandon it' option because the paragraph spends a sentence agreeing with the critics.",
   "V02E-body-mass-index", "Critique-of-a-critique structure: one wrong option states the critics' view, another the opposite overstatement; the correct answer must hold the individual and population distinction.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

ps("V02-E-012", "Health and medicine", "A second opinion is valuable as an independent test, which may complicate the decision.", "Difficult",
   "Patients are often told that seeking a second opinion is simply prudent, as if two doctors were always better than one. The benefit is real but depends on independence. A second doctor who reads the first doctor's notes, sees the same scans already labelled, and knows what the referring colleague concluded is likely to anchor on that conclusion, so agreement may reflect deference rather than confirmation. A genuinely independent opinion, formed before the first is known, is more informative precisely because it can disagree. Yet disagreement brings its own burden: the patient must now choose between experts without the knowledge to judge them. A second opinion is valuable less as a reassuring echo than as an independent test, and patients should be ready for it to complicate rather than settle the decision.",
   "A second opinion adds value chiefly when formed independently of the first, since only then can it truly test it, though such independence may leave the patient facing an unresolved disagreement.",
   [("Second doctors who read the first doctor's notes and labelled scans tend to defer to the referring colleague, so their agreement often reflects anchoring rather than confirmation.",
     "This is the supporting point of the third sentence; it omits the paragraph's conclusion about independence as the source of value and the burden that disagreement brings."),
    ("Since independent doctors are likely to disagree with one another, second opinions usually confuse patients and should be sought only when the first diagnosis seems doubtful.",
     "The paragraph says an independent opinion is 'more informative precisely because it can disagree' and asks patients to be ready for complication, not to avoid second opinions; the restriction is not in the text."),
    ("The value of a second opinion lies in the reassurance it offers, provided the second doctor reaches the same conclusion without having seen the first doctor's notes.",
     "The paragraph says a second opinion is valuable 'less as a reassuring echo than as an independent test'; making its value depend on agreement and reassurance reverses this, even though it keeps the independence condition.")],
   "The paragraph qualifies the advice that a second opinion is 'simply prudent': its benefit 'depends on independence', because a doctor who knows the first conclusion may merely defer. An independent opinion is informative because it can disagree, but disagreement leaves the patient a hard choice. The correct answer holds the condition, the reason and the cost.",
   "Choosing the reassurance option because it mentions independence; it keeps the condition but puts the value in agreement, the 'reassuring echo' the paragraph sets aside.",
   "V02E-second-opinions", "One wrong option keeps the paragraph's independence condition while reversing its account of where the value lies; another is an accurate but partial detail. Separating them needs the final sentence's contrast.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Cities and geography
ps("V02-E-013", "Cities and geography", "Desire paths are information about the routes people want, not vandalism.", "Easy",
   "In parks and on campuses one often sees a strip of bare earth cutting across a lawn, worn by walkers who ignored the paved path laid out beside it. Planners sometimes treat these desire paths as vandalism to be fenced off. They are better read as information. Each one records, more honestly than any survey, the routes people actually want to take, usually the shortest line between where they are and where they are going. Some universities have gone so far as to delay paving until they could see where the grass wore away, then laid paths along those lines. A design that people keep walking around is telling its makers something.",
   "Worn paths across lawns are honest evidence of the routes people want, not vandalism, and planners can use them to lay better paths.",
   [("Walkers who cut across lawns damage parks and campuses, so planners should fence off the grass to keep people on the paved paths provided for them.",
     "This is the planners' response the paragraph sets aside with 'They are better read as information'; it is the opposite of the paragraph's view."),
    ("Universities should never pave a campus until students have worn paths across its lawns, since surveys of walking habits are always unreliable guides to routes.",
     "The paragraph offers delayed paving as an example of what 'some universities' did, not a universal rule, and it says paths are more honest than surveys, not that surveys are always unreliable."),
    ("People walking through parks and campuses generally choose the shortest line between two points, even when a paved path is available close beside it.",
     "This is a supporting observation; it leaves out the paragraph's main claim that such paths are information that designers should heed.")],
   "The paragraph contrasts two readings of desire paths, vandalism and information, and argues for the second: the paths record the routes people actually want, and some planners have used them to place paths. Its closing line generalises the lesson for designers. The correct answer gives the contrast and the lesson.",
   "Choosing the shortest-line option, which is true in the paragraph but only a supporting detail.",
   "V02E-desire-paths", "The thesis is stated in the third sentence, and the wrong options are an opposite view, an over-generalised example and a detail.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-E-014", "Cities and geography", "Widened roads fill with trips congestion had suppressed, so lasting relief is rare.", "Medium",
   "Adding lanes to a jammed motorway looks like the plainest remedy for congestion, yet the relief it brings tends to be short-lived, and a few years on the road is typically as clogged as before. The reason is that congestion had been rationing use of the road. Once journeys speed up, drivers who had been taking other routes switch back, trips postponed to off-peak hours move to the rush hour, some commuters abandon trains for cars, and over a longer span households and employers settle farther apart. The new space is taken up by travel that the jams had been holding down. None of this makes extra capacity worthless, since more people now make journeys they value; it means only that widening is a poor tool for the lasting relief from congestion it is usually sold as delivering.",
   "New road capacity tends to fill with trips that congestion had deterred, so widening roads lets more people travel but seldom brings the lasting end to jams it promises.",
   [("Widening roads is pointless, since the new lanes are always as clogged as the old ones within a few years and nobody at all benefits from the extra capacity provided.",
     "The paragraph says 'None of this makes extra capacity worthless, since more people now make journeys they value', and it says the road is 'typically', not always, clogged again."),
    ("When roads become faster, people move farther from their jobs, so road widening is the main reason why cities have spread outward into the countryside.",
     "Households and employers settling farther apart is one of several responses listed; the paragraph makes no claim that widening is the main cause of urban spread."),
    ("Traffic jams persist after roads are widened because planners underestimate how quickly a city's population grows, adding new drivers faster than lanes can be built.",
     "The paragraph explains the refilling by changes in existing travel, 'travel that the jams had been holding down', not by population growth, which it never mentions.")],
   "The paragraph explains why widened roads soon clog again: congestion 'had been rationing use of the road', and faster journeys release the travel it was holding down. It then qualifies the point: extra capacity is not worthless, since more people make journeys they value, but widening is 'a poor tool for the lasting relief' it is sold as delivering. The correct answer states the mechanism and both halves of the qualification.",
   "Choosing the 'pointless' option, overlooking the concession that more people now make journeys they value.",
   "V02E-induced-traffic", "Explanation of a phenomenon with a concession; one wrong option ignores the concession and another substitutes a plausible outside cause.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-E-015", "Cities and geography", "Street names mostly work as addresses, weakening both sides; renaming concerns present expression.", "Difficult",
   "Arguments over renaming streets usually set two camps against each other. One holds that a name is a standing honour, and that keeping the name of someone whose deeds a city now condemns means continuing to honour them. The other holds that names are part of the historical record, and that erasing them tidies away an uncomfortable past. Both treat the name as if it had a single fixed meaning. In practice a street name works mostly as an address; few residents know whom their street commemorates, and the honour, if any, is faint. That faintness cuts both ways. It weakens the claim that the old name actively glorifies anyone, but it equally weakens the claim that removing it destroys history, which survives in archives and books rather than on signposts. The dispute is better seen as a contest over what a city chooses to say now than as a battle over the past.",
   "Since street names mostly function as addresses, they neither strongly honour anyone nor preserve history, which weakens both camps and makes renaming a question about present expression.",
   [("Because few residents know whom their street commemorates, old street names honour nobody at all, so cities can rename streets freely whenever their values change.",
     "The paragraph says the honour is 'faint', not absent, and that faintness 'cuts both ways'; using it to license renaming takes one side, which the paragraph declines to do."),
    ("History survives in archives and books rather than on signposts, so those who oppose renaming streets are mistaken while those who favour it are right.",
     "The paragraph uses the archive point against only one claim and weakens the other camp's claim equally, so it does not declare either camp right."),
    ("Debates over renaming streets are really about the past, since both camps agree that a name has a single fixed meaning which a city must either keep or erase.",
     "The paragraph reports that both camps treat a name as having a fixed meaning only to reject that assumption, and concludes that the dispute is about 'what a city chooses to say now' rather than the past.")],
   "The paragraph compares two camps, identifies their shared assumption that a name has 'a single fixed meaning', and replaces it: a street name works mostly as an address, so its honour is faint. That faintness 'cuts both ways', weakening both camps, and the dispute is recast as one about what a city says now. The correct answer holds the shared weakening and the recasting.",
   "Choosing an option that uses one of the paragraph's arguments against one camp, without seeing that the paragraph applies the same point symmetrically to both.",
   "V02E-street-renaming", "A comparison paragraph whose point is symmetry; two wrong options each take one valid argument and turn it into a verdict for one camp, and the third reports the shared assumption the author rejects.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=DX)

ps("V02-E-016", "Cities and geography", "Anti-sleeping street furniture displaces rather than solves and burdens everyone, so it is hard to justify as design.", "Medium",
   "Benches divided by armrests, ledges studded with metal knobs, and bus shelters with seats too narrow to rest on are usually defended as tidiness: they keep public spaces orderly and discourage people from sleeping in them. The defence describes the intent accurately but not the effect. Such designs do not reduce the number of people who have nowhere to sleep; they move them elsewhere, often to less safe places. Meanwhile they make the city less comfortable for everyone else who needs to sit, including the elderly, the pregnant and the tired. A measure that burdens many people while relocating rather than solving the problem it targets is hard to justify as good design, whatever its tidiness.",
   "Street furniture meant to stop people sleeping in public merely moves them elsewhere while making seating worse for everyone, so it is hard to justify as good design despite its aim of order.",
   [("City authorities install armrests and narrow seats chiefly out of hostility towards people without homes, which shows that public design is driven by prejudice.",
     "The paragraph says the tidiness defence 'describes the intent accurately', so it accepts the stated motive; attributing the designs to prejudice goes against the text."),
    ("Benches with armrests and seats that are too narrow are uncomfortable for the elderly, the pregnant and the tired, and so should be redesigned to meet the needs of these groups.",
     "This keeps one cost and adds a redesign prescription, leaving out the paragraph's central point that the designs relocate rather than solve the problem they target."),
    ("Such designs keep public spaces orderly and reduce the number of people sleeping rough, though they make seating somewhat less comfortable for other users.",
     "The paragraph says these designs 'do not reduce the number of people who have nowhere to sleep', so this option contradicts its main factual claim.")],
   "The paragraph accepts the stated intent of anti-sleeping designs but judges their effect: they relocate people rather than reduce their number, and they burden every other user. It concludes that such a measure is 'hard to justify as good design, whatever its tidiness'. The correct answer holds both effects and the verdict.",
   "Choosing the prejudice option, which sounds sympathetic to the paragraph's stance but contradicts its acceptance of the stated intent.",
   "V02E-hostile-street-furniture", "Critique of a defence; the tempting wrong option agrees with the author's verdict but for a reason the author explicitly does not give.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Philosophy and ethics
ps("V02-E-017", "Philosophy and ethics", "A theory that always requires the best act erases the category of admirable but optional acts.", "Difficult",
   "Some moral theories hold that we are always required to do the best act available to us. On such a view there is no room for heroism in the ordinary sense: the person who runs into a burning building to save a stranger has merely done their duty, and anyone who could have done so and did not has done wrong. Most of us find this hard to accept. We think there are acts that are good, even admirable, to do but not wrong to omit, and that praising the rescuer while not blaming the bystander is not a muddle but an accurate reflection of morality's shape. A theory that cannot make room for this category is not more rigorous than common sense; it simply erases a distinction that our practices of praise and blame rely on.",
   "A theory that always demands the best act cannot recognise acts that are admirable yet optional, and losing this distinction, on which praise and blame depend, is a defect rather than rigour.",
   [("Since heroic rescues go beyond what duty requires, people who decline to attempt them cannot be criticised in any way, whatever the risks and whatever the stakes.",
     "The paragraph says some admirable acts are 'not wrong to omit', but it makes no claim that declining any rescue is beyond all criticism 'whatever the stakes'; the option extends one example into a sweeping rule and drops the point about the theory."),
    ("Moral theories that demand the best available act are more rigorous than common sense, even though most people find their conclusions about heroism hard to accept.",
     "The last sentence denies exactly this: such a theory 'is not more rigorous than common sense; it simply erases a distinction'."),
    ("Common sense is a more reliable guide to morality than any theory, since our everyday practices of praise and blame reveal the true shape of our duties.",
     "The paragraph criticises one kind of theory on one point; it does not rank common sense above every theory, so the option is far too broad.")],
   "The paragraph sets out a theory requiring always the best act, shows its consequence (no room for heroism beyond duty), appeals to the intuition that some acts are admirable but optional, and concludes that a theory erasing this category is not 'more rigorous' but defective, since praise and blame rely on the distinction. The correct answer carries the argument and its conclusion about the theory.",
   "Choosing the 'common sense is more reliable' option, which agrees with the author's direction but generalises a single criticism into a claim about all theories.",
   "V02E-supererogation", "Abstract ethical argument; one wrong option is the author's direction stretched to all theories, another is a sweeping version of the intuition, so the correct answer must be found by scope.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=DX)

ps("V02-E-018", "Philosophy and ethics", "White lies are minor but not costless: they cheapen sincere praise where honesty matters.", "Medium",
   "White lies, such as praising a disappointing gift, are commonly defended as harmless kindnesses: no one is hurt and feelings are spared. The defence overlooks a cost that is spread thin and therefore hard to see. Each white lie slightly lowers the value of the speaker's sincere words, since a friend who always praises cannot be fully believed when the praise is real. Among people who know each other well, the convention is usually understood, and little is lost. But where honest feedback matters, a habit of comfortable falsehood can leave others unable to trust our approval when they most need to. White lies are not grave wrongs, yet calling them costless mistakes a small and diffuse harm for no harm at all.",
   "White lies are not grave wrongs, yet they are not costless, since habitual kindly falsehoods make genuine praise less credible where honesty matters.",
   [("White lies are serious moral wrongs, since every falsehood, however kind, damages trust and makes it impossible for others to believe anything the speaker says.",
     "The paragraph says white lies 'are not grave wrongs' and that each one only 'slightly' lowers the value of sincere words, so 'serious' and 'impossible' overstate it."),
    ("Among people who know each other well, white lies are understood as a convention, so they are harmless and spare feelings without any real cost.",
     "The paragraph concedes that little is lost among close acquaintances but rejects the claim that white lies are costless; this option turns the concession into the defence it criticises."),
    ("People should give honest reactions to disappointing gifts, since friends who receive candid feedback will learn to choose better presents for them in future.",
     "The paragraph makes no recommendation about gifts and never mentions better presents; it is about the credibility of praise, so this adds an outside idea.")],
   "The paragraph criticises the defence of white lies as 'harmless': each one slightly devalues sincere words, a cost that matters where honest feedback is needed. It concedes that little is lost among people who understand the convention and that white lies are 'not grave wrongs', but it rejects calling them costless. The correct answer keeps the cost, its mechanism and both limits.",
   "Choosing the 'convention' option because it repeats a sentence of the paragraph, not noticing that it is a concession the conclusion qualifies.",
   "V02E-white-lies", "One wrong option converts the paragraph's concession into its conclusion, and another overstates its criticism; the correct answer must match the degree.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

ps("V02-E-019", "Philosophy and ethics", "Mutually beneficial exchange can be exploitative through unfair division, yet banning it would not help.", "Difficult",
   "Suppose a firm offers work in a poor region at wages far below what it pays elsewhere, and people eagerly accept because the job is better than anything else available. Many would call this exploitation, yet the workers are better off, and had the firm stayed away they would be worse off still. Here a puzzle arises: the firm that exploits seems to act worse than a firm that never came, even though it does more good. The resolution is that wrongness can lie in how a benefit is shared, not only in whether one is conferred. Taking unfair advantage of someone's lack of options can be wrong even when the exchange helps them; but it does not follow that preventing such exchanges is right, since that would remove the benefit without correcting the unfairness.",
   "An exchange can be wrongly exploitative even though it benefits the weaker party, because unfairness concerns how gains are divided, yet banning such exchanges would not cure that unfairness.",
   [("A firm that pays low wages in a poor region acts worse than a firm that never comes there at all, so such firms should be prevented from operating on these terms.",
     "The paragraph says such a firm only 'seems' to act worse, presents this as a puzzle to resolve, and explicitly denies that 'preventing such exchanges is right'."),
    ("Because the workers are better off and accept the jobs eagerly, paying them far less than workers elsewhere cannot properly be called exploitation at all.",
     "The paragraph holds that taking unfair advantage 'can be wrong even when the exchange helps them', so it does not deny that the arrangement is exploitation."),
    ("Exploitation is wrong because it harms the weaker party, so the proper remedy is to require firms to pay workers in poor regions what they pay elsewhere.",
     "The paragraph locates the wrong in 'how a benefit is shared', not in harm, since the workers are better off; it also proposes no wage requirement.")],
   "The paragraph poses a puzzle: an exploitative firm seems worse than an absent one though it does more good. It resolves it by locating wrongness in the division of benefit rather than in harm, then adds that preventing such exchanges does not follow, since that removes the benefit without fixing the unfairness. The correct answer states the resolution and the qualification.",
   "Choosing the option that bans the exchanges, treating the 'seems to act worse' sentence as the author's conclusion rather than as the puzzle being resolved.",
   "V02E-mutually-beneficial-exploitation", "The paragraph moves from puzzle to resolution to a qualification; each wrong option stops at, reverses or misreads one stage, and the correct answer needs all three.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=DX)

ps("V02-E-020", "Philosophy and ethics", "Apology makes forgiveness fitting, but forgiveness need not wait for it.", "Medium",
   "A familiar view holds that forgiveness has to be earned: until the wrongdoer apologises, forgiving them merely condones the wrong. This view captures something, since an apology acknowledges the wrong and gives forgiveness something to answer. But it makes the victim's release from resentment hostage to the choices of the person who caused the harm. Someone wronged by a person who has died, vanished or refuses to admit fault would be left with no way to set the injury down. Forgiveness without apology need not deny that a wrong occurred or excuse it; it can mean only that the victim ceases to let the wrong govern their attitude. An apology makes forgiveness fitting, but its absence need not make forgiveness impossible or mistaken.",
   "Though an apology gives forgiveness something to answer, forgiveness need not wait for one, since it can free the victim from resentment without excusing the wrong.",
   [("Forgiveness must be earned through an apology, because forgiving someone who has not acknowledged a wrong amounts to condoning that wrong and excusing the wrongdoer.",
     "This is the view the paragraph reports in its first sentence and then argues against, on the ground that it makes the victim's release 'hostage' to the wrongdoer."),
    ("Victims of people who have died or vanished should always forgive them, since otherwise resentment will go on governing their lives indefinitely.",
     "The paragraph says forgiveness without apology is possible and need not be mistaken; it does not say victims should always forgive, so the obligation is an addition."),
    ("Apologies are of little moral importance, since forgiveness depends wholly on the victim's own choice and is unaffected by anything the wrongdoer says or does.",
     "The paragraph says an apology 'makes forgiveness fitting' and 'captures something', so dismissing apology as unimportant contradicts its concession.")],
   "The paragraph grants that an apology gives forgiveness 'something to answer', but argues that requiring one makes the victim's release 'hostage' to the wrongdoer, and that forgiveness without apology need not excuse the wrong. Its last sentence states the balance: apology makes forgiveness fitting, but its absence does not make forgiveness impossible. The correct answer keeps the concession and the main claim.",
   "Choosing the 'apologies are of little importance' option, which pushes the paragraph's argument past its own concession.",
   "V02E-forgiveness-without-apology", "Argument against a reported view with a concession; one wrong option is the reported view and another over-extends the author's side past the concession.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Psychology
ps("V02-E-021", "Psychology", "Remembered discomfort follows the worst and final moments, so memory and experience diverge.", "Easy",
   "When people look back on an unpleasant experience, such as a medical procedure or a long wait, their judgement of how bad it was seems to depend heavily on two moments: the worst point and the final stretch. How long the experience lasted counts for surprisingly little. One consequence is odd but well documented in laboratory settings: tacking a short stretch of gentler discomfort onto the end of an ordeal can make people remember the whole as less unpleasant, even though they suffered more in total. This tells us that remembered experience and lived experience can diverge. It does not settle which one matters more, and a doctor who prolonged a procedure merely to improve its memory would face a real ethical question.",
   "Memories of unpleasant experiences depend mainly on their worst and final moments rather than their length, so memory and lived experience can differ, though which matters more is left open.",
   [("Doctors should add a few minutes of milder discomfort to the end of medical procedures, since patients will then remember those procedures as less unpleasant.",
     "The paragraph says such a doctor 'would face a real ethical question' and that it does not settle which experience matters more, so it makes no such recommendation."),
    ("Because people remember past experiences inaccurately, their reports of earlier pain are worthless, and only measurements taken during an experience can be trusted.",
     "The paragraph says it 'does not settle which one matters more'; declaring remembered experience worthless decides the question it leaves open."),
    ("The longer an unpleasant experience lasts, the worse people remember it as being, unless the experience ends with a stretch of milder discomfort.",
     "The paragraph says duration 'counts for surprisingly little', so making length the main driver of memory contradicts it.")],
   "The paragraph reports that memories of discomfort are shaped by the worst point and the end rather than duration, gives the striking consequence of adding milder discomfort, and concludes that memory and experience can diverge, while leaving open which matters more. The correct answer keeps the finding and the open question.",
   "Choosing the duration option because it mentions the gentler ending, without noticing that it makes length the main factor.",
   "V02E-peak-end-rule", "The finding and its qualification are stated directly, and each wrong option clearly contradicts one sentence.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-E-022", "Psychology", "Plans overrun because forecasters picture steps and miss unlikely delays; start from similar projects.", "Medium",
   "People asked how long a task will take routinely underestimate, even when they have overrun on similar tasks many times before. The error persists because of how forecasts are made. Planners imagine the particular task in front of them, step by step, and each step looks manageable; the delays that actually ruin schedules, such as illness, interruptions or a supplier's mistake, are individually unlikely and so do not appear in the imagined sequence, even though some such delay almost always occurs. Past overruns are explained away as caused by special circumstances that will not recur. A more reliable forecast begins from the outside, by asking how long similar projects have actually taken, and only then adjusts for what is special about this one.",
   "Plans run late because forecasters picture a task's steps and overlook the many individually unlikely delays, so estimates should start from how long similar projects actually took.",
   [("People underestimate how long tasks will take because they are naturally optimistic and wish to impress others, so all forecasts should simply be doubled.",
     "The paragraph explains the error by the way forecasts are built, step by step, not by optimism or a wish to impress, and it recommends starting from similar projects, not a fixed multiplier."),
    ("Illness, interruptions and suppliers' mistakes are the main causes of delay in projects, and so planners should take steps to prevent them from happening.",
     "These are examples of delays that are 'individually unlikely'; the paragraph's point is that forecasts ignore them, not that they should be prevented."),
    ("Forecasts based on past projects are unreliable because every task has its special circumstances, so planners should picture each step of the task in front of them.",
     "This reverses the paragraph, which treats appeals to 'special circumstances' as the excuse that sustains the error and recommends beginning from how long similar projects took.")],
   "The paragraph explains why underestimates persist: forecasters picture the steps of one task, each looks manageable, and the individually unlikely delays that almost always occur are left out; past overruns are excused as special. It recommends starting from how long similar projects took. The correct answer states the mechanism and the remedy.",
   "Choosing the reversal, which uses the paragraph's own phrase 'special circumstances' but recommends the very method that produces the error.",
   "V02E-planning-fallacy", "Explanation of a phenomenon whose remedy is subtle; one wrong option inverts it using the paragraph's own wording.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

ps("V02-E-023", "Psychology", "Familiarity breeds liking gently and conditionally, not automatically.", "Easy",
   "Repeated exposure to something, whether a face, a tune or an unfamiliar word, tends to make people like it more, even when they do not consciously remember having encountered it. Familiarity seems to make a thing easier to process, and that ease is felt as a mild pleasure that is credited to the thing itself. The effect has limits, however. It is strongest for things that are neutral or mildly pleasant to begin with; repeated exposure to something disliked can deepen the dislike, and beyond a certain point repetition breeds boredom rather than affection. Familiarity, in short, is a gentle and conditional influence on liking, not a guarantee of it.",
   "Repeated exposure tends to raise liking because familiar things feel easier to process, but it works best on neutral things and can fail with dislike or overexposure.",
   [("The more often people encounter something, the more they will come to like it, whatever their first reaction to it and however many times they see it.",
     "The paragraph says the effect 'has limits': dislike can deepen and repetition can breed boredom, so 'whatever' and 'however many times' contradict it."),
    ("People like familiar faces and tunes because they consciously remember earlier pleasant encounters with them and connect those memories with comfort.",
     "The paragraph says liking grows 'even when they do not consciously remember having encountered it' and attributes it to ease of processing, so this mechanism contradicts the text."),
    ("Repeated exposure to disliked things deepens the dislike, and too much repetition breeds boredom, so familiarity generally harms rather than helps liking.",
     "These are the limits the paragraph lists, not its main claim; it calls familiarity a gentle influence that 'tends to make people like it more', so 'generally harms' reverses it.")],
   "The paragraph states the finding that exposure increases liking, explains it by ease of processing, and then gives its limits: it works best on neutral things and can reverse with dislike or overexposure. The final sentence sums up: a 'gentle and conditional influence'. The correct answer keeps the effect, its mechanism and its conditions.",
   "Choosing the 'more often, the more liked' option, which ignores the stated limits.",
   "V02E-mere-exposure", "A finding with explicitly flagged limits ('however', 'in short'); the wrong options are easy to rule out against single sentences.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-E-024", "Psychology", "We overestimate how much others notice our slips because we judge from inside.", "Easy",
   "After spilling coffee on a shirt or stumbling over a word in a meeting, people often feel that everyone noticed and will remember. Usually few did. This overestimate, sometimes called the spotlight effect, arises because each of us experiences our own appearance and actions from the inside, vividly and continuously, and we find it hard to adjust for the fact that others are busy with their own concerns. We anchor on our own intense awareness and correct too little. Knowing this will not make embarrassment vanish, but it can make it easier to act without excessive worry about a judgement that, for the most part, is not being made.",
   "People overrate how much others notice their slips because they judge from their own vivid self-awareness, and knowing this can ease, though not remove, worry about being judged.",
   [("Other people never notice our minor mistakes, so embarrassment about spilling coffee or stumbling over words is entirely irrational and should simply be ignored.",
     "The paragraph says 'Usually few did' notice and that the judgement is mostly 'not being made', which is not 'never'; it also says knowing this 'will not make embarrassment vanish'."),
    ("People are embarrassed by small mistakes because they care too much about the opinions of others, a habit that can be overcome through regular practice in public speaking.",
     "The paragraph explains the overestimate by judging from one's own vivid awareness, not by caring too much about opinions, and it never mentions public speaking practice."),
    ("Others are so busy with their own concerns that they rarely judge anyone at all, which is why most people feel very little embarrassment after making minor slips in public.",
     "The paragraph says people 'often feel that everyone noticed', so they do feel embarrassment; the option reverses the phenomenon it describes.")],
   "The paragraph describes the overestimate, explains it by our vivid inside view of ourselves and insufficient adjustment for others' preoccupation, and draws a limited lesson: knowing this can ease worry though it 'will not make embarrassment vanish'. The correct answer keeps the phenomenon, cause and limited lesson.",
   "Choosing the 'never notice' option, which turns 'usually few did' into an absolute.",
   "V02E-spotlight-effect", "The cause and the hedged lesson are stated plainly, and each wrong option clearly overstates, substitutes a cause or reverses the effect.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-E-025", "Psychology", "Self-handicapping protects a fragile sense of ability at the cost of making failure likelier.", "Medium",
   "A student who goes out the night before an important exam, or an athlete who skips training before a big race, may seem simply careless. Often something subtler is happening. By creating an obstacle in advance, the person secures an excuse: if they fail, the failure can be blamed on the late night rather than on a lack of ability, and if they succeed despite the handicap, their ability looks all the greater. The strategy protects a fragile sense of competence. Its price is that it makes failure more likely, and it tends to be adopted most by those who are least sure of their ability, the very people who could most use a fair test of it.",
   "Some people handicap themselves before tests to shield their sense of ability from failure, but this makes failure likelier and is commonest among those least sure of themselves.",
   [("Students who go out the night before exams and athletes who skip training are careless people who do not much care about the outcome of the tests they face.",
     "The paragraph says such people 'may seem simply careless' and that 'something subtler is happening': they care so much about their ability that they protect it."),
    ("Creating obstacles before a test is a shrewd strategy, since it lets people look more able when they succeed and gives them a ready excuse when they fail.",
     "This leaves out the paragraph's stated 'price', that the strategy 'makes failure more likely', and its point about who adopts it, so it misrepresents the author's view as approval."),
    ("People who are unsure of their ability perform badly in exams and races because anxiety prevents them from preparing as thoroughly as their more confident rivals.",
     "The paragraph describes a deliberate strategy of creating an obstacle to secure an excuse, not an anxiety that prevents preparation; anxiety as the cause is not in the text.")],
   "The paragraph reinterprets apparent carelessness as a strategy: creating an obstacle secures an excuse for failure and magnifies success, protecting 'a fragile sense of competence'. It then states the cost (failure becomes likelier) and the irony that the least confident use it most. The correct answer holds the motive, the cost and who adopts it.",
   "Choosing the 'shrewd strategy' option, which accurately describes the mechanism but omits the cost that makes the paragraph's view critical.",
   "V02E-self-handicapping", "One wrong option correctly states the mechanism but drops the cost; telling it from the correct answer needs attention to the paragraph's final evaluation.",
   cognitive="Analysis")
